# Shell scripts are read by a shell inside a Linux container, and a shebang
# ending in a carriage return fails as "bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M".
#
# The repository already stores them with LF, so CI is fine either way. The
# trap is local: core.autocrlf is on for this project, so a fresh checkout on
# Windows writes CRLF into the working tree, and `docker build` copies the
# working tree rather than the index. The image then builds cleanly and dies on
# first start, which is a long way from the cause.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The same argument, for the files a container reads as configuration or as an
# entrypoint by another name.
Dockerfile* text eol=lf
*.conf text eol=lf
*.template text eol=lf
